Oliver Wen wrote:
> Today we have updated versions of roguelikes - Diablo, Diablo II,
> Sacred and many many more.

Because, to be perfectly honest, those games SUCK.  This may be an
opinion, but it is one that has come from over a decade of exhaustive
thinking about Roguelikes and what make them interesting.  I do not say
this idly.

No commercial RPG (with the possible exception of the Genesis ToeJam &
Earl, oddly enough, or maybe one or two Japanese games) has yet to
adequately take the necessary lessons away from the original Rogue.
Even Diablo takes its lessons from Angband, which for its strengths is
a pale shadow of Nethack.

I don't think it's impossible to improve upon Nethack, but I find it
interesting how many point-missing attempts there have been to do just
that.  It's almost comical.

